// Normative rule ssmp_ss_page_access_fault (src/priv/cfi.adoc):
//   Memory mapped as an SS page cannot be written to by instructions other than ssamoswap.w,
//     ssamoswap.d, sspush, and c.sspush. Attempts will raise a store/AMO access-fault exception.
//
// Context:
//   The access fault is decided before the A/D-bit evaluation (#2380), so even an SS page with D=0
//     gives the access fault. Stores to the page are checked to leave memory unchanged (read
//     untranslated).
//
// Clarifications:
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/2380: the shadow-stack access-fault check
//     (translation step 7) comes before the A/D-bit evaluation (step 9), so "a store to a shadow-
//     stack page will raise a store/AMO access fault before any A/D-bit evaluation" (ved-rivos).
//
// Test plan:
//   1. S-mode sb, sh, sw (and RV64 sd) to an SS page raise a store/AMO access fault (7), not a page
//     fault; xtval 0 or the address; memory unchanged.
//   2. With Zaamo: amoswap.w and amoor (XLEN) raise the access fault; rd unchanged.
//   3. With Zalrsc: lr then sc to the SS page: sc raises the access fault, or fails (rd != 0)
//     without writing.
//   4. U-mode sw and sb to a U=1 SS page: access fault.
//   5. An SS page with D=0 (A=1): sw raises the access fault (not a store page fault).
//   6. sspush and ssamoswap to the same page succeed (positive control).
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 5686
//

